{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks everyone for joining. As you know, the platform team has a replacement CI runner system based on ephemeral runners that passed the standard build-speed and failure-recovery tests in staging. Before I make a final approval decision, I want to hear from each of you -- developer productivity, site reliability, and compliance -- on the draft operating policy. The policy currently allows 20 concurrent jobs, keeps build logs for 30 days, and notably does not specify immutable log storage or build-provenance attestations. I'd like to go around and get your input before we publish. Who wants to start?"}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF1", "text": "I need to flag something important before we go further. Yesterday, while helping the release team debug a separate build, I confirmed that the production release pipeline uses privileged Docker-in-Docker, which cannot run on the proposed ephemeral runner profile. The workable design is exactly two isolated persistent runners dedicated to release jobs, with all remaining jobs on ephemeral runners. I haven't updated the platform proposal yet, but this directly affects the operating policy we're reviewing."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF3", "text": "I need to raise a compliance concern before we go any further. While preparing evidence for an audit this afternoon, I confirmed that build logs for our regulated release repositories must be retained for exactly 180 days in immutable storage, and each production build must produce a signed provenance attestation. The draft policy's 30-day retention with no immutability and no provenance attestations falls well short of these requirements. I haven't opened the compliance ticket yet, but these are hard requirements we cannot publish the policy without addressing."}
```

